[0.005, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[H\u001b[6G\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mbe observable through the API.' How do you want to prove ready works? Options: (a) just GET the task and see status=ready after \r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m \u001b[3G \u001b[5G \u001b[38;2;153;153;153madding/completing deps, or (b) also expose next-task which is the canonical 'what's ready?' query. → Include next-task\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· I found a struct vs spec drift: ExportDocument in model.rs has `exported_at` and `knowledge_items`, but the OpenAPI schema uses \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B`knowledge` (no `exported_at`). There may be other small drifts. Should I fix these as I wire up handlers, or leave them for a \r\u001b[2C\u001b[1B\u001b[39m   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mseparate cleanup? → Fix as we go\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m· For dynamic contract conformance in integration tests (validating responses against openapi/shepherd.yaml), what's your \r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m     \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mpreferred approach? → oas3 + jsonschema\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;78;186;101m⏺\u001b[3G\u001b[39mAgent \"Explore shepherd codebase\" finished"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m · 3\u001b[50G 48s\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m⏺\u001b[3G\u001b[39mGood answers. One more critical question before I write the plan:\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\u001b[1BPlanning: \u001b]8;id=14o9qrt;file:///Users/sheplu/.claude/plans/we-are-working-on-swirling-hearth.md\u0007/Users/sheplu/.claude/plans/we-are-working-on-swirling-hearth.md\u001b[39m\u001b]8;;\u0007\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"]
[0.000, "o", "─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\u001b[1B\u001b[48;2;177;185;249m\u001b[38;2;0;0;0m ☐ Task actions \u001b[39m\u001b[49m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[2m│\u001b[3G\u001b[22m\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m\u001b[1mThe OpenAPI spec has 5 task action endpoints: approve, reject, block, unblock, cancel. The issue deliverables say 'approve/reject \r\u001b[1B\u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[2m│\u001b[3G\u001b[22m\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m\u001b[1mactions'. The store already has all 5 methods built. Should I wire up all 5 since they're trivial (~10 lines each), or strictly scope\r\u001b[1B\u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[2m│\u001b[3G\u001b[22m\u001b[38;2;255;255;255m\u001b[1mto approve+reject only?\r\u001b[1B\u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;177;185;249m❯\u001b[3G\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m1.\u001b[6G\u001b[38;2;177;185;249mAll 5 actions\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m \u001b[3G   \u001b[38;2;153;153;153mWire approve, reject, block, unblock, cancel. They're already built in the store, each handler is ~10 lines, and it completes the \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1Blifecycle story for contract testing.\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m \u001b[3G\u001b"]
[0.000, "o", "[38;2;153;153;153m2.\u001b[39m Only approve + reject\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mStrictly follow the issue deliverables. Block/unblock/cancel land in a later session.\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m  \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m3. Type something.\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m  4. Chat about this\u001b[K\r\u001b[3C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153mEnter to select · ↑/↓ \u001b[24Go navigate · Esc\u001b[41Gto cancel\u001b[39m\u001b[K\u001b[30;1H\u001b[21;1H\u001b[?2026l"]
